CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters mandated by the Special Act on Promotion of Offshore Wind Power Deployment and Industry Development and matters necessary for the enforcement thereof.

CHAPTER II OFFSHORE WIND POWER COMMITTEE AND OFFSHORE WIND POWER TASK FORCE

Article 2 (Composition of Offshore Wind Power Committee) (1) “Heads of central administrative agencies prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 7(2)1 of the Special Act on Promotion of Offshore Wind Power Deployment and Industry Development (hereinafter referred to as the "Act") means the Commissioner of the Korea Coast Guard and the Director of the National Intelligence Service.
  (2) A member commissioned under Article 7(2)2 of the Act may continue to perform his or her duties even after the expiration of the term of office prescribed in paragraph (7) of that Article, until a successor is commissioned.
  (3) “Cases prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as where the subject matter of the agenda item is minor” in Article 7(8) of the Act means any of the following cases:
  1. Where the subject matter of the agenda item is minor;
  2. Where there is insufficient time to hold a meeting attended by the members of the Offshore Wind Power Committee under Article 6(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "Committee") due to urgent circumstances;
  3. Where it is difficult to constitute a quorum for a meeting through the attendance of members due to a natural disaster or other unavoidable circumstances, or where it is deemed particularly necessary upon consultation between the two chairpersons under Article 7(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "co-chairpersons").

Article 3 (Operation of Committee) (1) A meeting of the Committee shall be convened jointly by the co-chairpersons, and the presiding chairperson of the meeting shall be determined upon consultation between the co-chairpersons.
  (2) To convene a meeting, the co-chairpersons shall notify each member of the date, time, venue, and agenda of the meeting in writing or via electronic documents at least 10 days prior to the meeting; provided, in cases of urgency or any unavoidable circumstances, such notice may be given by the day prior to the meeting.
  (3) The Committee may, where necessary for the performance of its duties, request relevant public officials or experts to attend its meeting and state their opinions.
  (4) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (3), matters necessary for the composition and operation of the Committee shall be determined by the co-chairpersons following deliberation and resolution by the Committee.

Article 4 (Composition and operation of working committee) (1) "Minor matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 7(9)2 of the Act means the following:
  1. Matters regarding modified approval of an implementation plan under Article 6(1)5 of the Act (limited to cases where the location and size of the offshore wind power project site and the capacity of offshore wind power equipment remain identical, and there is no change in the electric power system interconnection plan or the core infrastructure plan);
  2. Matters determined by the co-chairpersons following deliberation and resolution by the Committee, which are recognized as minor matters among those regarding dispute mediation under Article 6(1)9 of the Act.
  (2) The working committee under Article 7(9) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "working committee") shall consist of up to 25 members, including one chairperson.
  (3) The Vice Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment designated by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall serve as the chairperson of the working committee, whose members include the following persons:
  1. One person from each of the Ministry of Finance and Economy, the Ministry of National Defense, the Ministry of the Interior and Safety, the Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, the Ministry of Trade, Industry and Resources, the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment, the Ministry of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries, the Ministry of Planning and Budget, the Korea Heritage Service, and the Korea Coast Guard, who is designated by the head of each of the above agencies from among public officials belonging to the Senior Executive Service and public officials of the National Intelligence Service equivalent thereto;
  2. A person commissioned by the Prime Minister, taking gender into consideration, from among persons with extensive knowledge of and experience in offshore wind power, dispute mediation, or other related fields.
  (4) Where deemed necessary for deliberation, the working committee may request relevant public officials or experts to attend its meeting and state their opinions.
  (5) The term of office of a member of the working committee commissioned under paragraph (3)2 shall be 2 years, and the member may be reappointed only once. In such cases, the commissioned member may continue to perform his or her duties even after the expiration of his or her term of office, until a successor is commissioned.
  (6) No member of the working committee shall be dismissed or removed from office against his or her will, except in any of the following cases:
  1. Where the member is sentenced to imprisonment without labor or a heavier punishment;
  2. Where the member becomes unable to perform his or her duties for a long period due to a mental or physical weakness.
  (7) The working committee shall have one secretary to handle its administrative affairs, who shall be designated by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment from among public officials in general service belonging to the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment.
  (8) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (7), matters necessary for the composition and operation of the working committee shall be determined by the co-chairpersons following deliberation and resolution by the Committee.

Article 5 (Composition and operation of offshore wind power task force) (1) The offshore wind power task force under Article 10(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "task force") shall perform the following business affairs:
  1. Preparation of agenda items to be submitted to the Committee and the working committee;
  2. Monitoring of the status of implementation of the Committee’s recommendations for improvement under the former part of Article 6(2) of the Act;
  3. Other business affairs to support the smooth performance of duties of the Committee and the working committee.
  (2) The task force shall consist of one head and members.
  (3) The head of the task force shall be appointed by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment from among public officials in general service belonging to the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment.
  (4) The head of the task force shall oversee the business affairs of the task force under the direction of the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, and shall direct and supervise its members.
  (5) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (4), matters necessary for the composition and operation of the task force shall be determined by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment.

Article 6 (Designation of dedicated offshore wind power institution) (1) Pursuant to Article 11(1) of the Act, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may designate any of the following institutions or organizations satisfying the designation criteria provided in paragraph (2) as a dedicated institution for offshore wind power (hereinafter referred to as "dedicated institution"):
  1. The Korea Energy Agency established under Article 45 of the Energy Use Rationalization Act;
  2. The Korea Marine Environment Management Corporation under Article 96 of the Marine Environment Management Act;
  3. Other institutions or organizations recognized by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment as having expertise in the offshore wind power industry. 
  (2) The designation criteria for the dedicated institution shall be as follows:
  1. Having in place a dedicated organization and dedicated human resources necessary for performing the projects provided in the subparagraphs of Article 11(2) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as “projects of the dedicated institution”);
  2. Having in place the electronic data processing system necessary for performing the projects of the dedicated institution, and security measures for such system;
  3. Having in place internal regulations regarding the dedicated organization, dedicated human resources, the electronic data processing system, etc. provided in subparagraphs 1 and 2.
  (3) A person who intends to be designated as a dedicated institution shall submit an application for designation as a dedicated institution to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment.
  (4) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment designates a dedicated institution under Article 11(1) of the Act or revokes such designation under paragraph (4) of that Article, he or she shall post such fact on the website of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment.
  (5) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may request the head of the dedicated institution to submit data necessary for inspecting the performance of projects of the dedicated institution.
  (6) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may, based on the results of the performance inspection under paragraph (5), require the head of the dedicated institution to take necessary measures, such as operational improvements.

CHAPTER III DESIGNATION OF PRELIMINARY ZONES AND POWER GENERATION ZONES

Article 7 (Establishment and operation of offshore wind power site information network) (1) “Information prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as wind conditions, fishing activities, environment and marine environment, maritime traffic, military operations impact, national heritage impact, and information on electric power systems” in Article 12(1) of the Act means the following information necessary for the designation of a preliminary zone for offshore wind power generation (hereinafter referred to as “preliminary zone”) and an offshore wind power generation zone (hereinafter referred to as “power generation zone”):
  1. Wind conditions information;
  2. Fishing activities information;
  3. Environmental and marine environmental information;
  4. Maritime traffic information;
  5. Military operations impact information;
  6. National heritage impact information;
  7. Information on electric power systems; 
  8. Marine meteorological information;
  9. Information on the water depth of the sea area, the geology of the seabed, etc.;
  10. Other information recognized by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment or the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries as necessary for the designation of preliminary zones and power generation zones.
  (2) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall establish appropriate security measures to prevent the leakage of information held in and managed by the offshore wind power site information network under Article 12(1) of the Act.
  (3) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) and (2), matters necessary for the management of the offshore wind power site information network, provision of data and information, establishment of security measures, etc. shall be determined and publicly notified jointly by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 8 (Scope of date related to marine exploration and survey findings and marine site information) The scope of data related to the marine exploration and survey findings and marine site information shall be determined and publicly notified jointly by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 9 (Designation of preliminary zones) (1) "Requirements prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 14(1)7 of the Act means the following:
  1. The impact on national heritage or marine activities shall be minimal;
  2. There shall be no pending dispute regarding maritime boundaries between local governments.
  (2) The competent Special Metropolitan City Mayor, Metropolitan City Mayor, Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Do Governor, or Special Self-Governing Province Governor (hereinafter referred to as "Mayor/Do Governor") who intends to apply for the designation of a preliminary zone under Article 14(2) of the Act shall submit documents containing the following matters to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries, respectively:
  1. The location and size of the area, for which an application for designation as a preliminary zone is to be filed;  
  2. Grounds for applying for designation as a preliminary zone;
  3. Details of self-review on the requirements under the subparagraphs of Article 14(1) of the Act.
  (3) Upon receipt of an application under paragraph (2), the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall review whether the requirements under the subparagraphs of Article 14(1) of the Act are met. In such cases, if the application documents for designation of a preliminary zone require any supplementation, they may request the competent Mayor/Do Governor to make such supplementation within a specified period of up to 30 days.
  (4) Where an application filed under paragraph (2) (including where supplementation is made pursuant to the latter part of paragraph (3)) falls under any of the following cases, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may return the application:
  1. Where it is clear that the details of the application do not meet the requirements under the subparagraphs of Article 14(1) of the Act;
  2. Where a person fails to submit documents containing the matters under the subparagraphs of paragraph (2) or fails to comply with a request for supplementation under the latter part of paragraph (3).
  (5) “Modify any significant matter prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as the location and area of a preliminary zone” in Article 14(3) of the Act means that a modification is made to the location or area of a preliminary zone, resulting in a change of the Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu (referring to the head of an autonomous Gu; hereinafter the same shall apply) who shall establish and operate a public-private consultative body under Article 17(1) of the Act.

Article 10 (Formulation and finalization of basic design) (1) "Modification of significant matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 16(1) of the Act means any of the following cases:
  1. Where the area subject to the finalized basic design is increased by 10/100 or more;
  2. Where the capacity of offshore wind power equipment is increased by 10/100 or more;
  3. Where a substantial modification is made to an electric power system interconnection plan or a major infrastructure plan.
  (2) "Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 16(1)7 of the Act means the following:
  1. A preliminary survey plan and mitigation measures regarding natural ecology and living environments;
  2. A plan for the creation and operation of an offshore wind power demonstration complex under Article 37(1) of the Act (applicable only where such creation and operation are required).
  (3) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment conducts a marine environmental impact survey under the main clause of Article 16(3) of the Act, he or she shall prepare a survey report on the matters prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment.
  (4) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment has conducted an environmental impact survey under the proviso of Article 16(3) of the Act, he or she shall prepare a survey report on the matters prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment.
  (5) "Period prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the former part of Article 16(4) of the Act means 30 days.
  (6) Notwithstanding paragraph (5), where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries is unable to submit opinions within the relevant period due to unavoidable circumstances, he or she may extend the period only once by up to 20 days.
  (7) Except as provided in paragraphs (3) through (6), matters necessary for a marine environmental impact survey under the main clause of Article 16(3) of the Act and an environmental impact survey under the proviso of that paragraph shall be determined and publicly notified jointly by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 11 (Composition and operation of public-private council) (1) The public-private council under Article 17(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as “public-private council") shall consist of at least 15 but not more than 25 members, including 2 chairpersons, and the following persons shall jointly serve as the co-chairpersons:
  1. A person elected by and from among the members under paragraph (2)1 and 3;
  2. A person elected by and from among the members under paragraph (2)2;.
  (2) The members of the public-private council shall be commissioned or appointed by a Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu required to establish and operate a public-private council under Article 17(1) of the Act, from among the following persons; and in such cases, members falling under subparagraph 1 shall constitute at least 1/2 of the total number of members, and those falling under subparagraph 3 shall constitute at least 1/5 of the total number of members:
  1. Representatives of fishery organizations and resident representatives (including resident representatives of areas along the transmission line routes);
  2. Public officials of the relevant Special City, Metropolitan City, Special Self-Governing City, Do, or Special Self-Governing Province (hereinafter referred to as "City/Do") or Si/Gun/Gu (referring to an autonomous Gu; hereinafter the same shall apply);
  3. Persons with extensive knowledge of and experience in such fields as energy and resources, the environment and marine environment, the fishing industry, maritime traffic, land use, science and technology, electric power systems, national defense, accounting and finance, or conflict management.
  (3) The term of office of the members commissioned under paragraph (2)1 and 3 shall be 2 years; provided, the term of office of a member newly commissioned due to the resignation of a member or other reasons shall be the remainder of his or her predecessor's term.
  (4) Meetings of the public-private council shall be held when jointly convened by the 2 chairpersons under paragraph (1) or upon the request of at least 1/3 of the total members in office, and the chairpersons shall take turns presiding over such meetings.
  (5) A meeting of the public-private council shall be convened with the attendance of at least 2/3 of the total members in office, and its resolutions shall be passed by the concurring votes of at least 2/3 of the members present.
  (6) The public-private council may have subcommittees if necessary for the efficient operation of its business affairs.
  (7) Pursuant to the latter part of Article 17(2) of the Act, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may provide a Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu who establishes and operates the public-private council with support equivalent to that provided to an institution conducting a distribution project under Article 27(2) of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on the Promotion of the Development, Use and Diffusion of New and Renewable Energy, as publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment.
  (8) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (7), matters necessary for the establishment and operation of the public-private council and subcommittees shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment.

Article 12 (Hearing of opinions of stakeholders and experts) (1) Prior to consultations by the public-private council under Article 17(4) of the Act, a Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall give public notice of the following matters in the Official Gazette and on the website of the relevant Si/Do or Si/Gun/Gu, and shall make the draft basic design under subparagraph 1 available for review by stakeholders, such as fishers and residents, and by relevant experts for at least 14 days from the date of such public notice:
  1. A draft basic design;
  2. Period and methods of review;
  3. Methods of submitting opinions.
  (2) Any person who has an opinion on the draft basic design may submit such opinion to the Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu within the review period under paragraph (1)2.

Article 13 (Notification period for public-private council consultation results) (1) "Period prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the main clause of Article 17(5) of the Act means one year (or 6 months where a preliminary zone is designated under Article 14(2) of the Act) from the day following the date of receipt of the notification of a draft basic design under Article 16(5) of the Act.
  (2) Where the notification period for consultation results is extended under the proviso of Article 17(5) of the Act, the period of such extension shall not exceed 6 months.
  (3) The period spent on mediation of disputes, etc. under Article 17(6) of the Act shall be included in the period under paragraph (1) or (2).

Article 14 (Mediation of disputes over matters subject to public-private council consultation) (1) A Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu who intends to request the mediation of a dispute from the Committee under Article 17(6) of the Act shall submit an application for dispute mediation to the Committee, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment.
  (2) The Committee shall prepare a mediation proposal and recommend it to the relevant Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu within 45 days from the date of receipt of the application for dispute mediation under paragraph (1); provided, where the Committee intends to extend the period due to unavoidable circumstances, it shall notify the relevant Mayor/Do Governor or the head of the Si/Gun/Gu thereof by specifying the grounds and the period for such extension.
  (3) A Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu who has received a recommendation under paragraph (2) shall reconvene the public-private council for consultations on the mediation proposal, and shall notify the Committee of whether he or she consents to such mediation proposal within 30 days from the date of receipt of the recommendation.

Article 15 (Requirements for designation of power generation zones) (1) "Requirements prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 19(1)6 of the Act means the following:
  1. To consider the impact on national security;
  2. To consider the results of a marine environmental impact survey under the main clause of Article 16(3) of the Act and an environmental impact survey under the proviso of that paragraph.
  (2) “Modify significant matters prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as the location and area of a power generation zone” in Article 19(2) of the Act means any of the following cases: 
  1. Where the area of the power generation zone is to be increased by 10/100 or more;
  2. Where the capacity of offshore wind power equipment is to be increased by 10/100 or more;
  3. Where a substantial modification is to be made to an electric power system interconnection plan or a core infrastructure plan.

Article 16 (Scale of power generation zone subject to installation of joint connection equipment) (1) “Power generation zone of at least a certain scale prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the former part of Article 20(1) of the Act means one or more power generation zones wherein the total capacity of offshore wind power equipment exceeds 1,000 megawatts, and which the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment recognizes as necessary to install joint connection equipment under Article 20(1) of the Act, taking into account the distance between offshore wind power equipment, etc. 
  (2) “Equipment prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the former part of Article 20(1) of the Act means electric installations defined in subparagraph 16 of Article 2 of the Electric Utility Act, which are determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, taking into account the stable connection to the power grid, etc.
  (3) “Special circumstances prescribed by Presidential Decree” in the latter part of Article 20(1) of the Act means the following cases:
  1. Where it is technically impossible to install joint connection equipment due to reasons such as topographic or geological conditions of the relevant area;
  2. Where joint connection equipment already exist or are under installation.

CHAPTER IV SELECTION OF OFFSHORE WIND POWER PROJECT DEVELOPER AND APPROVAL OF IMPLEMENTATION PLAN

Article 17 (Matters for consideration in selecting offshore wind power project developer) (1) "Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 24(1)5 of the Act means the following:
  1. Efforts to ensure national and energy security;
  2. Safety assurance efforts for development projects in power generation zones; 
  3. Efforts to minimize impacts on the environment, including the marine environment;
  4. Cost feasibility of the offshore wind power project;
  5. Offshore wind power facility decommissioning and site restoration plan.
  (2) "Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 24(2)4 of the Act means the following:
  1. A basic design finalized under Article 16(1) of the Act;
  2. Results of consultations by the public-private council under Article 17(5) of the Act;
  3. Schedule for the selection of an offshore wind power project developer;
  4. Matters required for bidding, including costs payable by offshore wind power project developers under Article 32(1) of the Act.

Article 18 (Selection of offshore wind power project developer) (1) A person who intends to be selected as an offshore wind power project developer under Article 24(1) of the Act shall submit an application for selection as an offshore wind power project developer to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment.
  (2) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may establish and operate a selection committee for offshore wind power project developers (hereinafter referred to as "selection committee") if necessary for the professional and technical review of applications for selection under paragraph (1).
  (3) The selection committee shall consist of up to 15 members, including one chairperson. 
  (4) The chairperson of the selection committee shall be elected by and from among its members, and the members shall be appointed by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment from among persons with extensive knowledge of and experience in offshore wind power.
  (5) The term of office of the members of the selection committee shall be 2 years, and they may be reappointed only once; provided, if the selection committee is dissolved under paragraph (6), their terms of office shall be deemed to expire at the time of such dissolution.
  (6) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may dissolve the selection committee where he or she deems that the purpose of establishment thereof has been achieved.
  (7) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (6), procedures for selecting an offshore wind power project developer and matters necessary for the establishment and operation of the selection committee shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment.

Article 19 (Approval of implementation plan) (1) "Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 25(1)12 of the Act means the following:
  1. Matters regarding the implementation of the results of a marine environmental impact survey under the main clause of Article 16(3) of the Act and an environmental impact survey under the proviso of that paragraph;
  2. Matters regarding the implementation of the results of an environmental assessment under Article 26 of the Act;
  3. The following matters regarding offshore wind power facilities:
  a. Design documents and technical specifications of major equipment;
  b. Construction schedule;
  c. Technical review report on the stability and adequacy of offshore wind power equipment;
  4. Matters regarding safety measures for marine activities;
  5. Matters regarding the decommissioning of offshore wind power facilities and site restoration.
  (2) An offshore wind power project developer who intends to obtain approval or modified approval of an implementation plan under Article 25(1) of the Act or the main clause of Article 25(4) of the Act shall submit to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment an application for approval of an implementation plan or an application for modified approval of an implementation plan, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment.
  (3) "Modification of any minor matter prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the proviso of Article 25(4) of the Act means any of the following cases:
  1. Modification of the name, address, and contact information of an offshore wind power project developer (in the case of a corporate entity, its name, address, and contact information and the name, address, and contact information of its representative);
  2. Correction of miscalculations, clerical errors, omissions, or any other clear errors equivalent thereto;
  3. Modification of the matters under paragraph (1)3a (limited to cases where the location and area of the offshore wind power project site and the capacity of the offshore wind power equipment remain the same, and there are no changes to the electric power system interconnection plan and core infrastructure plan);
  4. Modification of any of the following matters falling under subparagraph 3;
  a. Matters regarding the implementation period of the offshore wind power project under Article 25(1)3 of the Act (limited to changing such period by not more than one year);
  b. Matters regarding funds required for the implementation of the offshore wind power project under Article 25(1)4 of the Act and financing thereof.
  (4) "Period prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the latter part of Article 25(5) of the Act means 30 days.
  (5) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment grants approval or modified approval of an implementation plan under Article 25(1) of the Act or the main clause of Article 25(4) of the Act, he or she shall publicly notify the relevant matters in the Official Gazette as classified below:
  1. Approval of an implementation plan: The following matters:
  a. The name of the power generation zone, and the name and address of the offshore wind power project developer (in the case of a corporate entity, its name and the name and address of its representative);
  b. Overview of the implementation plan under Article 25(1) of the Act;
  c. Location and area of the offshore wind power project site;
  d. A detailed statement of the land, buildings, other objects fixed on the land necessary for an offshore wind power project under Article 29(1) of the Act, and rights other than ownership thereto, mining rights, fishing rights, aquaculture rights, and rights to use water (only applicable where relevant);
  2. Modified approval of an implementation plan: Any modified matter among the matters prescribed in the items of subparagraph 1.

Article 20 (Submission of preparatory statement for environmental assessment) (1) Prior to an environmental assessment under Article 26(1) of the Act, an offshore wind power project developer may submit to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment a preparatory statement for an environmental assessment prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment, and request the Minister to determine the items, scope, methods and other relevant details of such environmental assessment (hereinafter referred to as "assessment items, etc.").
  (2) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment receives a request under paragraph (1), he or she shall send the preparatory statement for an environmental assessment to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries, and may organize and operate an environmental assessment council (hereafter in this Article referred to as "assessment council") if necessary to determine the assessment items, etc. In such cases, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall consult with the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries regarding the organization and operation of the assessment council.
  (3) The assessment council shall consist of up to 10 members, including one chairperson.
  (4) The chairperson of the assessment council shall be designated by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment from among the public officials under his or her supervision, and the members of the assessment council shall be appointed or commissioned by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment from among those with extensive knowledge of and experience in environmental assessment and those recommended by a Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu who establishes and operates a public-private council under Article 17(1) of the Act.
  (5) The term of office of the members of the assessment council shall be 2 years, and they may be reappointed only once; provided, if the assessment council is dissolved under paragraph (6), their terms of office shall be deemed to expire at the time of such dissolution.
  (6) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may dissolve the assessment council where he or she deems that the purpose of establishment thereof has been achieved.
  (7) When determining assessment items, etc., the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall ensure that the environmental assessment does not overlap with the marine environmental impact survey under the main clause of Article 16(3) of the Act and the environmental impact survey under the proviso of that paragraph, and shall determine the assessment items, etc., within the necessary scope.
  (8) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall notify the offshore wind power project developer of the assessment items, etc. determined under paragraph (7).
  (9) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (8), matters necessary for the formulation of a preparatory statement for environmental assessment, the composition and operation of the assessment council shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment.

Article 21 (Environmental assessment procedures) (1) An offshore wind power project developer shall conduct an environmental assessment in accordance with Article 26(1) of the Act regarding the assessment items, etc. notified under Article 20(8), prepare an environmental assessment report prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment, and submit the report to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. In such cases, the offshore wind power project developer may seek the opinions of a Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu responsible for organizing and operating a public-private council under Article 17(1) of the Act before submitting the environmental assessment report.
  (2) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, who has received an environmental assessment report under Article 26(1) of the Act, and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries, who has been requested to consult on the environmental assessment report under the former part of paragraph (2) of that Article, shall review its appropriateness and other matters and may request an offshore wind power project developer to supplement the report, if necessary. In such cases, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall request, through the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, the offshore wind power project developer to supplement the report.
  (3) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries, who has been requested to consult on the environmental assessment report under the former part of Article 26(2) of the Act, shall submit his or her opinion to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment within 30 days from the date of receiving such request. In such cases, public holidays, Saturdays, and the period required for the offshore wind power project developer to supplement the report at the request of the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries made through the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment under the latter part of paragraph (2) shall not be included in the period referred to in the former part.

Article 22 (Revocation of approval of implementation plan) (1) "Reasons prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 28(1)3 of the Act means any of the following cases:
  1. Where a power generation zone development project is promoted differently from the implementation plan approved, or approved as modified, under Article 25(1) or (4) of the Act, thereby causing a material impediment to the performance of the project;
  2. Where approved matters and approval conditions are violated, or the performance thereof is impossible;
  3. Where construction for a power generation zone development project has commenced without filing a report under Article 30(1) of the Act, or where such construction is suspended for 3 months or longer;
  4. Where a material hazard to the environment, marine environment, or maritime safety has occurred or is highly likely to occur.
  (2) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment revokes the approval of an implementation plan under Article 28(1) of the Act, he or she shall notify the following matters in the Official Gazette:
  1. The name and address of the offshore wind power project developer (in the case of a corporate entity, its name and the name and address of its representative);
  2. Grounds for the revocation of approval of an implementation plan;
  3. Date of the revocation of approval of an implementation plan.

Article 23 (Reporting on commencement of construction for development project) (1) An offshore wind power project developer who intends to commence a development project under Article 30(1) of the Act shall submit a report on the commencement of construction to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment.
  (2) An offshore wind power project developer who intends to suspend construction for 3 months or longer under Article 30(1) of the Act shall submit a report on the suspension of construction to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment.
  (3) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment receives a report under paragraphs (1) and (2), he or she shall notify the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries, as well as the competent Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu, of such fact.

Article 24 (Authorization for completion of power generation zone development project) (1) An offshore wind power project developer who intends to apply for authorization for completion under Article 30(1) of the Act shall submit an application for authorization for completion to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment.
  (2) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment grants authorization for completion under the main clause of Article 31(2) of the Act, he or she shall publicly notify the following matters under paragraph (3) of that Article:
  1. The name of the power generation zone, and the name and address of the offshore wind power project developer (in the case of a corporate entity, its name and the name and address of its representative);
  2. Overview of the offshore wind power project;
  3. Location and area of the offshore wind power project site;
  4. Date of authorization for completion.
  (3) An offshore wind power project developer who intends to obtain permission for use under the proviso of Article 31(5) of the Act shall submit to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment an application for permission for use before authorization for completion is granted, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment.

Article 25 (Costs payable by offshore wind power project developer) (1) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment conducts in advance development activities required to be performed by an offshore wind power project developer under Article 32(1) of the Act, the items subject to the imposition of costs payable by the developer shall be as follows:
  1. Installation of facilities required for the offshore wind power project, including wind measuring devices;
  2. Formulation and modification of a draft basic design under Article 16(1) of the Act;
  3. A marine environmental impact survey under the main clause of Article 16(3) of the Act and an environmental impact survey under the proviso of that paragraph;
  4. Other development activities directly related to the designation of preliminary zones and power generation zones, which are determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment.
  (2) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall calculate the total amount of costs payable by an offshore wind power project developer by aggregating the costs under the subparagraphs of paragraph (1); provided, where two or more offshore wind power project developers are selected for a single power generation zone, the total amount of such costs shall be calculated in proportion to the capacity of the offshore wind power equipment of each offshore wind power project developer.
  (3) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment recognizes that it is difficult for an offshore wind power project developer to pay the costs under Article 32(1) of the Act in a lump sum, he or she may allow the developer to pay such costs in installments, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment.

CHAPTER V PROMOTION OF OFFSHORE WIND POWER INDUSTRY AND SUPPORT FOR AFFECTED INDUSTRIES

Article 26 (Fostering of specialized human resources) (1) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment formulates policies necessary for fostering human resources specialized in the offshore wind power industry or conducts education and training for such specialized human resources under Article 35(1) and (2) of the Act, he or she shall consider the demand for specialized human resources in each of the following fields:
  1. Feasibility analysis for the offshore wind power project;
  2. Design of core materials and components, and systems related to the offshore wind power industry;
  3. Design, construction, operation, maintenance, decommissioning, and recycling of offshore wind power facilities;
  4. Other fields recognized as necessary by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment for the promotion and development of the offshore wind power industry.
  (2) In order to efficiently conduct education and training for specialized human resources under Article 35(2) of the Act, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may conduct such education and training in linkage with education and training courses operated by the head of a relevant central administrative agency or the head of a relevant institution or organization.
  (3) Matters necessary for the details, methods, etc. of education and training under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be determined by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment.

Article 27 (Creation and operation of offshore wind power demonstration complexes) (1) When selecting the site for an offshore wind power demonstration complex under Article 37(1) and (2) of the Act, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall consider the following matters:
  1. Feasibility of installing demonstration facilities and infrastructure for the verification of offshore wind power technologies;
  2. Accessibility to harbors, harbor facilities, and other supporting facilities under the Harbor Act.
  (2) “Relevant institutions prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as public institutions under the Act on the Management of Public Institutions” in the former part of Article 37(3) of the Act means the following:
  1. A public institution under Article 4 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions;
  2. A special purpose company established through investment by one or more public institutions under subparagraph 1 (limited to cases where the total equity held by the relevant public institutions exceeds 50/100 of the total equity).

Article 28 (Designation of specialized research institutions) (1) A person who intends to be designated as a specialized research institution under Article 38(1) of the Act shall submit an application for designation as a specialized research institution to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment.
  (2) The requirements for the designation of a specialized research institution under Article 38(1) of the Act shall be as follows:
  1. Having in place a research support infrastructure, including research personnel, facilities, and equipment, for the research and development of offshore wind power-related technologies and commercialization thereof;
  2. Having a track record of performance in the research and development of offshore wind power-related technologies and commercialization thereof;
  3. Having appropriate plans for the research and development of offshore wind power-related technologies and commercialization thereof;
  4. Being capable of establishing and operating a cooperative system encompassing industry, academia, and research institutes, etc. related to offshore wind power.

Article 29 (Support for fisheries and related industries) "Specialized institutions and organizations prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 41(3) of the Act means the following institutions and organizations:
  1. Schools under the subparagraphs of Article 2 of the Higher Education Act, which have established a research institute in the field of offshore wind power;
  2. The National Federation of Fisheries Cooperatives under subparagraph 5 of Article 2 of the Fisheries Cooperatives Act;
  3. The Korea Fisheries Resources Agency under Article 55-2 of the Fishery Resources Management Act;
  4. The Korea Fisheries Infrastructure Public Agency under Article 57 of the Fishing Village and Fishery Harbors Act;
  5. The Korea Maritime Institute established under Article 8 of the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Research Institutes;
  6. The Korea Institute of Ocean Science and Technology under the Korea Institute of Ocean Science and Technology Act;
  7. The Korea Maritime Transportation Safety Authority under the Korea Maritime Transportation Safety Authority Act;
  8. The Korea Hydrography and Research Association under Article 54 of the Act on Marine Research and the Use of Marine Information;
  9. The Korea Marine Environment Management Corporation under Article 96 of the Marine Environment Management Act;
  10. Local public enterprises under the Local Public Enterprises Act, which are determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment.

CHAPTER VI SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 30 (Entrustment of business affairs) (1) Pursuant to Article 46 of the Act, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may entrust the following business affairs to a dedicated institution:
  1. Support for the formulation and modification of a draft basic design under Article 16(1) of the Act;
  2. Review of the details of approval or modified approval of an implementation plan under Article 25(1) or (4) of the Act;
  3. Review and verification of the details of authorization for completion, and conduct of a completion inspection under Article 31.
  (2) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment entrusts business affairs under paragraph (1), he or she shall give public notice of the entrusted institution and the details of such entrusted affairs.

Article 31 (Re-examination of regulations) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall examine the appropriateness of the following matters every 3 years, counting from January 1, 2026 (referring to the period that ends on the day before the base date of every third year) and shall take measures, such as making improvements:
  1. Matters for consideration in selecting an offshore wind power project developer under Article 17(1);
  2. Matters to be included in an implementation plan under Article 19(1);
  3. Grounds for the revocation of approval of an implementation plan under Article 22(1).

